WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
Tonight, you climb into bed,

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:02.810
adjust your pillow,

3
00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.190
and discover that one corner of the blanket
has somehow escaped.

4
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.870
An annoying problem. But a fairly small one.

5
00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:11.700
Now remove the bed.

6
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.370
Remove the roof.

7
00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.360
Remove the walls.

8
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.440
Keep the approaching night.

9
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.510
Where are you sleeping?

10
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.200
Under that tree?

11
00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.080
Beside the water?

12
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.340
Inside the cave?

13
00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:29.010
And once you pick a spot, what exactly goes
between you and the ground?

14
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.930
Ancient people faced versions of these
questions in very different places.

15
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.840
Archaeologists have even found traces of the
beds they prepared.

16
00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:42.600
To understand those choices, let's give our
little traveller a simple task:

17
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.280
find somewhere to sleep before dark.

18
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.020
He would like to begin immediately.

19
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.040
Preferably by lying down.

20
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.720
First, imagine three possible spots.

21
00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.470
One is beautifully flat.

22
00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.730
Unfortunately, it is also wet.

23
00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.780
Another has an excellent view.

24
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.700
The wind seems to enjoy it too.

25
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.780
The third offers some shelter, but the ground
is covered in stones.

26
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.620
This is an imaginary campsite, but the
trade-offs are easy to understand.

27
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.240
A roof does not make a sharp stone
comfortable.

28
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.200
A soft surface does not stop the rain.

29
00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:20.780
And a place that looks pleasant in daylight
can feel very different after dark.

30
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.770
You also need to consider the rest of your
day.

31
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:25.660
Where is the water?

32
00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.490
Where are you finding food?

33
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.600
Where are the other people staying?

34
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.370
We cannot recover every decision an ancient
family made.

35
00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.540
But we can examine the spaces people used,

36
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.180
the things they brought into them, and the
surfaces they prepared.

37
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.667
That gives us a better starting point than
simply

38
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.480
pointing at a cave and declaring: bedroom.

39
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.400
Our traveller reluctantly gets back up.

40
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.080
A cave is one possibility.

41
00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.250
People also built shelters.

42
00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.520
At Ohalo Two, beside the Sea of Galilee,

43
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.620
archaeologists uncovered the remains of a camp
about twenty-three thousand years old.

44
00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:08.910
Its inhabitants fished, hunted, gathered
plants, and lived in huts made from
vegetation.

45
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.530
Inside one hut, researchers found deliberately
arranged grass bedding on the floor.

46
00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.250
They reconstructed a space with a hearth,
working areas,

47
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.610
and a likely sleeping area near the walls.

48
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.060
Picture the hut from above.

49
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.520
Different activities occupy different parts of
the same small space.

50
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.350
Somewhere to work.

51
00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.670
Somewhere for the fire.

52
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.870
Somewhere to settle down.

53
00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:39.260
There are no separate bedrooms in that
reconstruction, but there is organization.

54
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:45.260
For our traveller, this adds a useful
possibility: a sleeping place can be made.

55
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.800
You can modify the space around you instead of

56
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.460
waiting for the landscape to provide the
perfect room.

57
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.160
The hut also leaves us with a more interesting
question.

58
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.133
Once you have a place to lie down,

59
00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.040
how do you make the floor worth lying on?

60
00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:03.087
For that, jump much farther back, to Border
Cave in southern Africa.

61
00:03:04.597 --> 00:03:09.407
Researchers identified traces of grass bedding
dating to around two hundred thousand years
ago.

62
00:03:10.327 --> 00:03:12.467
People had brought bundles of grass into the
cave

63
00:03:12.527 --> 00:03:14.447
and spread them over layers of ash.

64
00:03:15.287 --> 00:03:18.367
A sleeping surface, assembled from available
materials.

65
00:03:19.177 --> 00:03:20.957
The ash is especially interesting.

66
00:03:21.677 --> 00:03:26.477
The researchers proposed that it helped create
a cleaner base and discourage crawling
insects.

67
00:03:27.347 --> 00:03:29.707
Some came from older bedding that had been
burned,

68
00:03:29.867 --> 00:03:32.627
suggesting that these surfaces were maintained
and renewed.

69
00:03:33.457 --> 00:03:36.627
The grass-covered areas also served as places
to work.

70
00:03:37.357 --> 00:03:39.687
A bed could be part of a shared living
surface,

71
00:03:39.967 --> 00:03:41.437
used differently through the day.

72
00:03:42.277 --> 00:03:44.727
We cannot ask its makers how comfortable it
felt.

73
00:03:45.587 --> 00:03:47.997
But the remains show deliberate preparation.

74
00:03:48.947 --> 00:03:52.247
Our traveller looks down at the bare patch of
ground he selected earlier.

75
00:03:53.167 --> 00:03:55.177
It now seems to be missing a few things.

76
00:03:56.107 --> 00:03:58.077
He returns with an armful of grass.

77
00:03:58.307 --> 00:03:59.057
Then another.

78
00:03:59.757 --> 00:04:02.017
His bedroom has developed a construction
phase.

79
00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:06.623
Unfortunately, comfort can attract complaints
from creatures much smaller than you.

80
00:04:07.503 --> 00:04:11.703
Imagine settling onto your carefully prepared
bedding, closing your eyes,

81
00:04:11.973 --> 00:04:13.633
and immediately needing to scratch.

82
00:04:14.343 --> 00:04:17.093
At Sibudu, a rock shelter in South Africa,

83
00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:20.900
bedding from about seventy-seven thousand
years ago included stems

84
00:04:20.953 --> 00:04:23.283
and leaves of grass-like plants called sedges.

85
00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:27.663
On top were leaves from a tree known as river
wild-quince.

86
00:04:28.393 --> 00:04:31.593
Those leaves contain chemicals with
insecticidal properties.

87
00:04:32.573 --> 00:04:35.933
Researchers interpret their presence as
evidence that people may

88
00:04:36.003 --> 00:04:38.253
have selected plants to help control pests.

89
00:04:39.083 --> 00:04:42.453
The choice of leaf could matter alongside the
choice of location.

90
00:04:43.473 --> 00:04:46.353
Later layers at the same site show bedding
being burned,

91
00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:49.563
interpreted as a way of maintaining the
occupied space.

92
00:04:50.193 --> 00:04:52.883
That does not reveal a universal cleaning
schedule.

93
00:04:53.443 --> 00:04:56.613
It reveals people repeatedly managing the
places where they lived.

94
00:04:57.573 --> 00:05:00.103
Our traveller inspects a leaf with new
respect.

95
00:05:00.953 --> 00:05:04.903
Then he inspects the tiny visitor on his ankle
with considerably less respect.

96
00:05:05.793 --> 00:05:09.123
Finding somewhere to sleep was only the
beginning of keeping it usable.

97
00:05:09.633 --> 00:05:11.400
There is another part of a sleeping place that

98
00:05:11.463 --> 00:05:14.813
will never appear in a pile of grass: the
people around it.

99
00:05:15.663 --> 00:05:19.983
To explore that, we need to leave prehistory
and look at a modern study.

100
00:05:21.083 --> 00:05:25.543
Researchers measured night-time activity among
Hadza hunter-gatherers in Tanzania.

101
00:05:26.463 --> 00:05:29.800
Across twenty days, the monitored participants
were all classified

102
00:05:29.863 --> 00:05:33.293
as asleep at the same time for just eighteen
minutes in total.

103
00:05:34.073 --> 00:05:37.473
People fell asleep, woke up, and stirred at
different times.

104
00:05:38.223 --> 00:05:40.673
Their sleep schedules also varied with age.

105
00:05:41.453 --> 00:05:44.667
The researchers suggested that this overlap
could provide opportunities

106
00:05:44.733 --> 00:05:47.393
for someone to notice a disturbance during the
night.

107
00:05:48.313 --> 00:05:50.443
These measurements came from movement
monitors.

108
00:05:51.173 --> 00:05:55.373
They do not establish that somebody was
consciously standing guard every minute.

109
00:05:56.083 --> 00:05:59.623
And the Hadza are a present-day community with
their own history.

110
00:06:00.433 --> 00:06:03.523
Their sleep cannot simply be copied into an
ancient camp.

111
00:06:04.193 --> 00:06:07.113
The study does illustrate a possibility worth
considering:

112
00:06:07.753 --> 00:06:12.123
sleeping among other people can create a very
different situation from sleeping alone.

113
00:06:12.943 --> 00:06:16.173
Our traveller quietly moves his bedding a
little closer to the group.

114
00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.077
Now the sun is setting.

115
00:06:18.677 --> 00:06:20.767
Does everybody immediately fall asleep?

116
00:06:21.587 --> 00:06:24.997
Modern research gives us a reason to question
that familiar picture.

117
00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:28.957
A study of three communities living without
industrial lifestyles

118
00:06:29.157 --> 00:06:31.533
found that participants generally began
sleeping

119
00:06:31.587 --> 00:06:33.187
several hours after sunset.

120
00:06:33.987 --> 00:06:36.157
Their sleep also varied with the season.

121
00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:39.607
That gives us examples of life without a
modern bedroom,

122
00:06:40.017 --> 00:06:43.647
while leaving the exact bedtime of a person at
Border Cave unknown.

123
00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:49.967
Archaeology preserves fragments: a floor, a
hearth, a layer of plants.

124
00:06:50.947 --> 00:06:55.667
From those fragments, researchers build
explanations with different levels of
certainty.

125
00:06:56.577 --> 00:07:00.957
They can identify carefully arranged bedding
without knowing who occupied each patch.

126
00:07:01.947 --> 00:07:06.447
They can reconstruct a likely sleeping area
without recovering a single dream.

127
00:07:07.297 --> 00:07:10.233
Our cartoon traveller has taken us between
places separated

128
00:07:10.297 --> 00:07:13.017
by enormous distances and stretches of time.

129
00:07:14.097 --> 00:07:17.927
The real people in those places had their own
surroundings, materials, habits,

130
00:07:18.117 --> 00:07:19.507
and problems to solve.

131
00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:22.763
So how did ancient people choose where to
sleep?

132
00:07:23.453 --> 00:07:26.603
There was no single answer across all of
prehistory.

133
00:07:27.583 --> 00:07:30.703
The surviving evidence shows people using
natural shelter,

134
00:07:31.003 --> 00:07:32.353
building living spaces,

135
00:07:32.533 --> 00:07:33.783
preparing plant bedding,

136
00:07:34.043 --> 00:07:36.293
and maintaining the surfaces they occupied.

137
00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:40.493
Choosing somewhere to sleep could involve
several connected decisions:

138
00:07:40.763 --> 00:07:41.393
the place,

139
00:07:41.603 --> 00:07:42.303
the shelter,

140
00:07:42.583 --> 00:07:43.923
the material underneath you,

141
00:07:44.283 --> 00:07:45.483
and the people nearby.

142
00:07:46.433 --> 00:07:49.853
Back in our imaginary camp, the traveller is
finally ready.

143
00:07:50.683 --> 00:07:52.053
He has considered the weather.

144
00:07:52.813 --> 00:07:54.233
He has found a sheltered patch.

145
00:07:55.003 --> 00:07:56.713
He has prepared a surface to lie on.

146
00:07:57.533 --> 00:07:58.733
The others are close by.

147
00:07:59.623 --> 00:08:01.363
He lowers himself onto the bedding.

148
00:08:02.073 --> 00:08:03.123
Shifts his shoulder.

149
00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:05.913
Rearranges a handful of grass.

150
00:08:06.683 --> 00:08:07.663
Closes his eyes.

151
00:08:08.433 --> 00:08:09.773
Then opens them again.

152
00:08:10.753 --> 00:08:12.693
Something is pressing into his back.

153
00:08:13.633 --> 00:08:17.533
He reaches underneath himself and pulls out
one extremely ordinary stone.

154
00:08:18.513 --> 00:08:21.403
After all that planning, there is still one
final adjustment.

155
00:08:22.683 --> 00:08:24.043
He puts the stone aside,

156
00:08:24.283 --> 00:08:25.233
lies down again,

157
00:08:25.563 --> 00:08:26.963
and finally gets comfortable.
